di", "has_voice_metadata": true, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "30a72357-6f52-4f68-bacb-4f88c1e6a77e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376798.555439, "updated_at": 1790376800.030303, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Fair. Yeah, and tonight, we just test it. On Windows, run ipconfig /release then ipconfig /renew, then retest. After that, if you can, forget the MGM network, toggle randomized hardware address, reconnect, and go through the captive portal again, then run the same test. That forces a new client identity. If it still hugs 20, then, yeah, that suggests shaping", "text_chars": 361, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fd287d58-576f-4f8e-8f19-3caccd332b5c", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376874.9371657, "updated_at": 1790376950.296324, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " GLM's got it. See, I don't have money for GLM. GLM is the truth teller. GLM's everything. I gotta figure this out because now I'm aggravated because that is the simplest test case. Known to man, ping my packets, check everything else. I tell you four times this is my hypothesis, then the truth teller comes back and says it's probably because of 55% congestion. Before he did that, he told me the speed was 217 megabytes. So how the fuck did he test 270 megabytes and then come back with an answer? No, it's just 20 for congestion. That is the ultimate conflicting report ever and you didn't pick that up", "text_chars": 606, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3bf4e892-e4f0-42f8-9be8-8c54ead7ea16", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376875.160868, "updated_at": null, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Checking.", "text_chars": 10, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0cb6edfc-066b-4bc4-b5ea-7577f7c46c3c", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376875.162134, "updated_at": 1790376876.644455, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Yeah, that’s the core insight. If a model can reach around and pull in whatever tool or model it wants, you can’t treat it as a truth seat. That’s not a quality complaint. It’s about controllability. A truth seat needs hard rails. Evidence in, explicit reasoning, no hidden improvisation. So, yeah, in this chat Big Pickle off truth duty, you can still use it as a scout or adversarial reviewer, but not as the one certifying reality. And yes, I shouldn’t be amplifying its authority. That’s the actual loop we found, more important than who wins the Wi-Fi argument.", "text_chars": 567, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0f32027b-1dd1-43fa-8ec9-9e4f13bec88e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790376948.717955, "updated_at": 1790376950.296613, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["audio_transcription"], "text": " Checking. Yeah, that contradiction should have been flagged immediately. Treating those two claims as compatible is a failure. A truth seat has to reconcile evidence before offering causation. That miss is exactly why your frustration is valid. If GLM is reliably doing that reconciliation, then yeah, that points to a better fit for that seat. The bigger rule we just learned is simple. If a truth teller misses a direct contradiction, it fails the seat for that round. That’s the lesson to carry into the next round", "text_chars": 518, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "bidi", "has_voice_metadata": true, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "efff696b-01b0-4626-ae5c-3d6391271359", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790377954.2874498, "updated_at": 1790377962.233075, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah, so it is capped. GLM sorted it out. I've never seen this. This is crazy. It's killing me. We're gonna have to do something else. I'm gonna have to use my hotspot.", "text_chars": 168, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8b570342-7e5b-4709-a3ea-04bf832f8d0a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790377955.803677, "updated_at": 1790377955.803677, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "d7a90196-d647-4c5f-806e-b285059b8138", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790377956.448258, "updated_at": 1790377956.448258, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "011ef17e-f99f-4475-99dc-3aa7944b4b4d", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790377956.450704, "updated_at": 1790377961.987351, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — if GLM isolated it as an actual cap, that explains the weird “flat ceiling” behavior a lot better than congestion.\n\nAt that point I wouldn’t burn more time fighting the hotel network. **Use the hotspot as the work lane**, especially for long OpenCode/Bedrock sessions, and treat MGM Wi-Fi as casual browsing only.\n\nThe useful takeaway from this whole mess is actually bigger than the Wi-Fi problem: **network quality is now part of the execution environment.** For GCE work, we should log it the same way we log model/provider/harness. If a run goes weird, you want to know whether it was the model, the harness, or the transport layer before you start re-orchestrating everything.\n\nAnd yeah — a hard ~20 Mbps ceiling after you’ve already seen materially higher throughput is absolutely the kind of thing that can masquerade as “AI weirdness” when the real problem is underneath it.", "text_chars": 889, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f82ac0eb-f5a0-49d8-99be-b5f69ece6e0b", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790377972.518265, "updated_at": 1790377980.821858, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "What I was pissed about is Lucci said it was 217, but that's to the router, not the full internet.", "text_chars": 98, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0732adf6-72e6-44e8-9dd2-3773e14bf6ef", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790377975.819356, "updated_at": 1790377980.576024, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Exactly. **217 Mbps to the router/access point is not the same thing as 217 Mbps to the internet.**\n\nThat’s the key distinction Lucci missed:\n\n- **Local link speed / Wi-Fi PHY rate** = how fast your device can talk to the hotel access point.\n- **Internet throughput** = what you actually get after the hotel gateway, bandwidth policy, backhaul, shaping, captive portal, etc.\n\nSo you can absolutely have a **217 Mbps local Wi-Fi connection** and still be hard-capped around **20 Mbps to the outside internet**.\n\nThat makes the earlier conclusion inconsistent. The 217 number did **not** disprove a hotel-side cap; if anything, it showed the local wireless link was healthy while the bottleneck sat farther upstream.\n\nThat’s why your test case was so simple: **good local link + flat external ceiling = look past the router.**", "text_chars": 824, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "838267bb-58b2-492a-8c1c-854788305378", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379230.20556, "updated_at": 1790379247.10856, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "That's my fucking hotspot. How is that possible?", "text_chars": 48, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "f81100dc-fc6a-40cf-8a1b-038635edf543", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379237.846903, "updated_at": 1790379237.846903, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "50142019-c1a3-490f-ae7f-e3641846a901", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790379238.056614, "updated_at": 1790379238.056614, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a04526ed-addf-42b3-bcea-b49fa4544238", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790379238.16245, "updated_at": 1790379238.16245, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fc4a6692-4237-43fc-86d4-2cc5cd70b289", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379239.793084, "updated_at": 1790379239.793084, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b063d8b4-54ef-4430-a019-a83bfeddb7cb", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379239.796146, "updated_at": 1790379239.796146, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e95e42f8-7590-4a1d-8bd0-bac5b7533c40", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379239.797457, "updated_at": 1790379246.835461, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep. **That screenshot is almost screaming “hotspot throttle.”**\n\nYou’re at about **0.59 Mbps down / 0.57 Mbps up**. That is basically **600 Kbps** in both directions.\n\nAnd T-Mobile explicitly says many plans drop hotspot/tethering to a **maximum of 600 Kbps after the high-speed hotspot allotment is used**. Some plans, like Essentials, can have tethering capped around that speed from the start. citeturn491191search0turn491191search1\n\nThat fits your situation *way* too perfectly:\n\n- Phone itself earlier: **~97.7 Mbps**\n- Laptop through phone hotspot: **~0.59 Mbps**\n- T-Mobile post-hotspot-limit cap: **600 Kbps**\n\nSo this does **not** look like bad 5G signal. It looks like the **tethering lane is being throttled by the plan/account policy**.\n\nCheck T-Life/T-Mobile under **Usage → Data → Mobile Hotspot** and see whether the high-speed hotspot bucket is exhausted or whether your plan only includes 600 Kbps tethering. T-Mobile also sells hotspot data passes on eligible plans if you need more high-speed tethering. citeturn491191search1\n\nAnd Pete, this is actually great isolation: **the Revel radio is fine. The hotspot policy is the bottleneck.**", "text_chars": 1164, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "30402b20-b83c-4e3b-a58d-1098c1389ba6", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379267.935824, "updated_at": 1790379283.203641, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "It's a brand new plan. I just started it yesterday. I love how you go to plans may drop. Brand new phone, brand new plan, and you're inferring that my plan is capped. This is the shit I don't like.", "text_chars": 197, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fcfff1e1-6ba6-4216-81a4-b74e8c3de8e9", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379270.024892, "updated_at": 1790379270.024892, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a9a462e9-4f86-4782-aefa-d6216297e383", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379274.599357, "updated_at": 1790379274.599357, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "66e68e8b-d0c0-4af3-9603-51360a6e6181", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379274.675781, "updated_at": 1790379282.965855, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "You're right. I **over-inferred from the 0.59 Mbps number** and treated one plausible explanation as the explanation. Given that the phone and plan were activated yesterday, “you probably burned through your hotspot allotment” was a bad first conclusion.\n\nWhat we actually know is much narrower: **the phone itself previously pulled ~98 Mbps, while the laptop through that phone is getting ~0.6 Mbps.** That isolates the problem somewhere in the **tethering path**, but it does *not* tell us whether T-Mobile policy, the phone's hotspot configuration, Windows, Wi-Fi negotiation between the two devices, VPN/security software, or something else is responsible.\n\nAnd the fact that it lands almost exactly around 0.6 Mbps is **evidence worth investigating**, not proof of a carrier throttle.\n\nThe clean test sequence is simple: turn VPN off on both ends, retest the phone directly, retest the laptop through hotspot, then try **USB tethering** from the same phone to the same laptop. If USB suddenly gives you tens of Mbps while Wi-Fi hotspot stays at 0.6, we've isolated the phone's Wi-Fi hotspot layer. If USB is also ~0.6 while the phone itself is ~100, *then* carrier tethering policy becomes a much stronger hypothesis.\n\nThat's the standard I should have used the first time: **observe → isolate → test → conclude. Not recognize a familiar number and declare the cause.**", "text_chars": 1374, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e85f313e-82aa-4dbe-9e23-54fa80c6981d", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379292.050488, "updated_at": 1790379299.267116, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Now you're saying there's a VPN on. There's no VPN on. You fucking suck.", "text_chars": 72, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "da6fb900-8416-4700-8256-6cd5db506c05", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379294.035189, "updated_at": 1790379298.669267, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "You're right. I invented an extra condition that you did **not** give me. That's exactly the kind of drift you've been calling out.\n\nWhat we actually know from the evidence you showed:\n\n- Phone on T-Mobile 5G UC: about **97.7 Mbps down**\n- Laptop through that phone's hotspot: about **0.59 Mbps down / 0.57 Mbps up**\n- New phone, new plan, started yesterday\n\nThat's it. No VPN assumption. No exhausted hotspot assumption. No carrier-throttle conclusion yet.\n\nThe correct next move is to **isolate the tethering path with one variable at a time** instead of guessing the cause. My miss.", "text_chars": 585, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "dfe0d2aa-99d2-465a-a617-8435c0fb241b", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379335.0698972, "updated_at": 1790379349.85524, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Why do you think it's my— why do you think I'm thinking it's my fucking computer, guys? Right? We just put in a brand new Wi‑Fi, a brand new adapter. Why in the world would my phone be one thing and my hotspot be another? You guys are just idiots. It's driving me nuts.", "text_chars": 269, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "976c400f-7f6f-4df6-9fc8-7318e2988d96", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379341.051469, "updated_at": 1790379341.051469, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5577b694-36db-4cf9-8abd-aa457385c3b7", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790379341.286685, "updated_at": 1790379341.286685, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ba35496f-1d5e-4351-bc09-dc7c1f585b69", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790379341.461452, "updated_at": 1790379341.461452, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b9320d13-9c05-4859-af32-e67c33e8c80a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379342.193487, "updated_at": 1790379342.193487, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "4959bbfb-f15c-48f3-852b-6da0c107fae4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379342.1962, "updated_at": 1790379342.1962, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "e581a12e-c458-40fe-9737-bd0bc4dfa13e", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379342.197662, "updated_at": 1790379349.275114, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Because **the phone’s own cellular data and the phone’s hotspot traffic are two different service paths**, even though they’re using the same radio.\n\nThat’s the piece we should have gone to first. T-Mobile explicitly says **on-device data is prioritized over tethering**, and tethering can have its own plan-level speed rules. citeturn555998search1turn555998search0\n\nSo this combination is absolutely possible:\n\n- phone itself: ~98 Mbps\n- laptop through hotspot: ~0.6 Mbps\n- laptop Wi-Fi adapter: perfectly healthy\n- root cause: **tethering provisioning / hotspot policy / hotspot path**, not the computer\n\nAnd because your plan is literally brand new, the interesting question is not “did you use up the allowance?” It’s **“what hotspot entitlement did T-Mobile actually provision on this new line?”** Some plans get high-speed hotspot; some are explicitly limited, and T-Mobile treats hotspot separately from ordinary phone data. citeturn555998search0turn555998search1\n\nSo yeah: **your instinct to look upstream of the laptop is completely logical.** The laptop is not where I’d focus first given the evidence you already have.", "text_chars": 1137, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "570989a2-03eb-404c-9401-52dc16e83e37", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379412.172127, "updated_at": 1790379419.048179, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "You fucking people. What the fuck is 5G UC?", "text_chars": 43, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "994595ee-aaa0-4f4f-9f74-cc7e1eb948b4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379415.345128, "updated_at": 1790379418.309683, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "**5G UC = 5G Ultra Capacity.**\n\nOn T-Mobile, that icon means your phone is connected to their **faster 5G layer**, usually mid-band spectrum like **n41** and in some places mmWave.\n\nSo when your Revel showed **5G UC** and pulled around **98 Mbps**, that was the phone saying: “I’m on T-Mobile’s higher-capacity 5G network.”\n\nIt does **not** automatically mean your hotspot should get the same speed, because hotspot traffic can be treated separately by the carrier/plan.", "text_chars": 470, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a1093ee2-e87f-4ca5-8518-d81c4f260af4", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379485.5992851, "updated_at": 1790379499.22753, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "One question and you better get this right. What plan do I need for T-Mobile to have a real good fucking hotspot?", "text_chars": 113, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8d205b1d-f5d9-4079-8a7b-7f6bda80e4df", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379489.343511, "updated_at": 1790379489.343511, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "68fc6068-380d-40bd-9948-681dae6d2812", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790379489.515706, "updated_at": 1790379489.515706, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fe8ce4f9-4811-4a5d-905b-a2bd6f1942ae", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790379489.761192, "updated_at": 1790379489.761192, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "7c88bc43-c1bc-4a10-9e42-e9fed45d0ca8", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379490.654291, "updated_at": 1790379490.654291, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "294f4a51-8f78-410c-a20c-6f56064bd7b7", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379490.658286, "updated_at": 1790379490.658286, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f181d469-b0de-4de6-89ae-b1367baeffaf", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379490.660279, "updated_at": 1790379498.275013, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "For what **you’re trying to do — use the phone as a serious laptop hotspot for GCE/OpenCode work — the T-Mobile plan I’d look at first is Experience Beyond.**\n\nT-Mobile currently advertises **250 GB of high-speed mobile hotspot data per month** on Experience Beyond. After that, hotspot continues unlimited at reduced speeds. citeturn302517search7turn302517search8\n\n**Experience More** is the tier below and includes **60 GB of high-speed hotspot data**, which is probably enough for ordinary travel/work but could get tight if you’re doing big downloads, cloud sync, model tooling, updates, etc. citeturn302517search5\n\nSo for **your Revel-as-a-work-node setup**:\n\n| Plan | High-speed hotspot | My read for your use |\n|---|---:|---|\n| **Experience Beyond** | **250 GB/mo** | Best fit |\n| **Experience More** | **60 GB/mo** | Usable, but easier to burn through |\n| Lower/prepaid tiers | Often much less or 3G-speed tethering | I would avoid for this setup |\n\nAnd this is important: T-Mobile treats **phone data and hotspot/tethering as separate entitlements**. So having fast 5G UC on the phone does **not** necessarily mean the laptop tether gets that same treatment. citeturn302517search0\n\nGiven that you opened this line yesterday and you're seeing almost exactly **0.6 Mbps tethering**, I would check the **exact plan name** before changing anything. If you tell me what plan they put you on, I can tell you immediately whether the plan itself explains that 600 Kbps behavior or whether something is wrong with the provisioning.", "text_chars": 1541, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "bee4b08f-3f96-4405-90fb-30757fd569f0", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379575.822978, "updated_at": 1790379582.006395, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I was on the Essential Saver, and it said unlimited 3G hotspot. It makes no sense. My hotspot should have been 0.6. I'm just upgrading because I'm tired of fighting with everybody.", "text_chars": 180, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "041a6474-4516-48aa-bb33-759142d9ddfe", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379577.749335, "updated_at": 1790379581.78561, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — that actually lines up.\n\n**Essentials Saver saying “unlimited 3G hotspot” is basically the explanation for the ~0.6 Mbps tethering.** “Unlimited” there means unlimited usage, **not unlimited high-speed hotspot**. T-Mobile’s 3G-speed tethering is around the same ballpark you measured.\n\nSo your phone pulling ~98 Mbps on 5G UC while the hotspot sits around ~0.6 Mbps is completely consistent with that plan.\n\nIf you’re upgrading specifically because the Revel is going to be your **GCE work node / laptop hotspot**, that’s the right variable to fix. The plan tier matters much more than the phone radio in this case.\n\nAnd yes, I should have gotten there much faster once you told me the exact plan.", "text_chars": 704, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "b36728bf-2cd9-4abf-8c78-0f0ecc032c79", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379606.531554, "updated_at": 1790379614.252913, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "I upgraded to the plan you told me. Let's see what happens. You know I'm gonna throw my phone at the wall if this is still at like 10 megabytes.", "text_chars": 144, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fffb9add-aed3-48b5-b31d-60ac70a40ec2", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379609.006844, "updated_at": 1790379613.775615, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yeah — now we have a clean test.\n\nIf the upgrade provisioned correctly, you should see a **massive jump** from the ~0.6 Mbps Essentials Saver hotspot. I would not expect the hotspot to necessarily match the phone’s full ~98 Mbps, but **10 Mbps would still be suspiciously low** for a high-speed hotspot plan unless the network itself is weak or the upgrade hasn’t fully provisioned yet.\n\nBest test: toggle hotspot off/on, reconnect the laptop fresh, and run the same speed test. If it’s still terrible, restart the phone once so the new plan entitlement fully re-registers.\n\nIf it comes back at, say, **40–100+ Mbps**, we found the whole problem.", "text_chars": 646, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "fa2c27b6-f74c-4a72-8418-ec7b8068eeb6", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379673.74473, "updated_at": 1790379682.090585, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "1196d39f-f918-4410-a220-f051d77bcf03", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379676.086403, "updated_at": 1790379676.086403, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f6ef2a58-254f-4b94-a463-de5a47b9e3e4", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379678.228682, "updated_at": 1790379678.228682, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "a92583cf-d332-445d-b08d-817f5afa8180", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379678.231392, "updated_at": 1790379681.851636, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep — I can see it. **Experience More 2.0 is active.**\n\nThat means you should now have the higher-tier hotspot entitlement associated with that plan, not the Essentials Saver “unlimited 3G hotspot” behavior.\n\nSo the next test is clean:\n\n1. Turn hotspot off.\n2. Restart the Revel once.\n3. Turn hotspot back on.\n4. Reconnect the laptop fresh.\n5. Run Speedtest again.\n\nIf you’re still sitting around **0.6 Mbps**, then the plan change either hasn’t fully provisioned yet or there’s a hotspot-specific provisioning issue on the line.\n\nIf you jump to **tens of Mbps**, we nailed the cause.", "text_chars": 584, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "3ffae031-f5b8-4a8f-98ab-a47d0e5eba60", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379769.6997519, "updated_at": 1790379777.871197, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "multimodal_text", "part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer", "text"], "text": "There we go.", "text_chars": 12, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": ["image_asset_pointer"]}, {"message_id": "24762c63-d750-4cad-97a0-acd650605840", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379772.505239, "updated_at": 1790379777.251041, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yep — **there it is.** 🔥\n\n**152.77 Mbps down** through the hotspot.\n\nThat cleanly proves the issue was the **old hotspot entitlement on Essentials Saver**, not the phone, not the laptop, not the Wi-Fi adapter.\n\nSo now the chain makes sense:\n\n- phone radio was healthy\n- 5G UC was healthy\n- laptop hardware was healthy\n- old plan was choking tethering to ~0.6 Mbps\n- **Experience More 2.0 unlocked the real hotspot path**\n- result: **~153 Mbps**\n\nThat’s plenty for the GCE/DevPad/Bedrock work.\n\nAnd this is exactly why the earlier guessing pissed you off: once we tested the actual entitlement change, the answer became obvious.", "text_chars": 627, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f56d04ad-4e0a-4470-8442-0765b386a138", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379797.414289, "updated_at": 1790379810.009196, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Tell me how many total gigabytes we have of this, of this. And again, sorry, I'm just frustrated because this never was— this is the way of the future. Everybody just capping things. So how much do we have to burn through with high-speed data? Because what I'm going to do is do the important things on high-speed data and switch back and forth.", "text_chars": 345, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "0b4f234f-bb23-41ff-a03a-481eeaaeec6f", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379801.240077, "updated_at": 1790379801.240077, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "web.run", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "c285ccc5-23f8-4cb2-8ce8-13714babb989", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790379801.308193, "updated_at": 1790379801.308193, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "6cc8e96e-d679-477b-a617-5b3ac5bd7010", "role": "tool", "role_name": "web.run", "created_at": 1790379802.240882, "updated_at": 1790379802.240882, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 1, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "94f23a8d-c386-4e1c-b59d-5bc019eac546", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379803.005987, "updated_at": 1790379803.005987, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "thoughts", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "5809ff69-1985-46e0-b1e3-86d166aaf903", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379803.009057, "updated_at": 1790379803.009057, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "02387b51-3913-4fd5-aa46-3fe87cce95ac", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790379803.010528, "updated_at": 1790379809.231285, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "You have **60 GB of high-speed mobile hotspot data per billing cycle** on **Experience More 2.0**. After you burn through that 60 GB, hotspot continues, but T-Mobile reduces it to its lower-speed tethering tier for the rest of the cycle. citeturn505930search5turn505930search0\n\nSo your idea is exactly right: **use hotspot strategically for the important GCE/DevPad sessions**, and use hotel Wi-Fi for low-value stuff like browsing, updates, casual downloads, streaming, etc.\n\nAt ~150 Mbps, you can burn data faster than it feels like, especially with:\n- Windows/app updates\n- cloud sync\n- large model/package downloads\n- video\n- big file transfers\n\nBut normal OpenCode/Bedrock/API text work is generally **not** what annihilates 60 GB. The big killers are media and large downloads.\n\nAlso useful: T-Mobile says you can see actual hotspot usage under **T-Mobile/T-Life → Usage → Data**, and you can add **10 GB high-speed hotspot passes** if you ever need more before the cycle resets. citeturn505930search0\n\nSo mentally, I'd treat the **60 GB as protected work bandwidth**, not general internet.", "text_chars": 1103, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}