n structure: session header, session footer, anchor, mid-flight state, appendices, explicit handoffs, and work-order boundaries.  \n> - **Clone structure, not history.** Do not copy old live handoffs or unresolved instructions into the Ad Hoc bootstraps.  \n> - One active identity/bootstrap at a time per execution lane.  \n> - Ad Hoc seats may investigate, build, test, peer-review, and close routine work within their delegated scope.  \n> - Ad Hoc work does **not** silently acquire Super Team authority.  \n> - When work reaches a material architecture, methodology, truth, governance, or production-signoff threshold, create an explicit handoff upward to the corresponding Super Team seat.\n>\n> **Operating model:**  \n> Ad Hoc Team = cheap/fast groundwork, experimentation, surgical implementation, routine validation, POCs, cleanup.  \n> Super Team = authoritative architecture, methodology, governance, material truth verification, production-grade signoff.\n>\n> **Primary objective for this conversation:**  \n> Turn the existing Rookie Pad into the first **GCE Ad Hoc DevPad**. Define the minimum taxonomy, identities, bootstrap template, routing rules, escalation gates, and handoff format needed to run small work cleanly without Don manually cycling through core GCE seats.\n>\n> **Important constraint:**  \n> Do not redesign the existing GCE framework. This is a parallel execution layer built from its proven patterns. The goal is to reduce orchestration tax and prevent bootstrap contamination, not create another sprawling governance system.\n>\n> **Success condition:**  \n> Don can open one Ad Hoc DevPad, give it a bounded work order, let the Ad Hoc team execute through the required roles, and only involve the GCE Super Team when a defined escalation threshold is hit.\n\nThat captures the thing we just discovered without turning the new pad into another giant framework project.", "text_chars": 2971, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], 